#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124109 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "da9684b4-df8d-531d-a200-cf54685067c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-10913" :question/problem "Consider the set $M$, depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1930/Source/Images/d5db148bd9b96019566e84915f993c5b.png)\n\n1. $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n2. $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n3. $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0$. A *neighborhood* of a point $\\mathbf x_0$ is the set of points whose distance from $\\mathbf x_0$ is less than $\\delta$.\n$\\{\\mathbf x: \\mid \\mathbf x-\\mathbf x_0 \\mid < \\delta \\}$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ is the open disc $\\mid \\mathbf x - \\mathbf x_0 \\mid < \\delta$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1930/Source/Images/762837d488d6392a90ad3e9d9e0b3fbb.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$. The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S$.\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S$.\nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1930/Source/Images/606518c44f3238e9f489156e526e1479.png)\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M$.\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M$.\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is an interior point because there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124110 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "7e78e3a1-836a-53af-95a3-0a9d192cc7d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-10924" :question/problem "Consider the set $[MATH: M=(-\\infty, 2]]$ and the points $A$, $B$, and $C$, shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1930/Source/Images/62389af9576e377c5c81474e27347e4f.png)\n\n1. $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n2. $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n3. $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0$. A *neighborhood* of a point $x_0\\in\\mathbb R$ is the set of points whose distance from $x_0$ is *smaller than* $\\delta{:}$\n$\\{x: \\mid x- x_0 \\mid < \\delta \\}$\n\nA neighborhood of $x_0$ is given by the open interval $(x_0-\\delta, x_0+\\delta)$, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1930/Source/Images/87c93f9fa4ef79f3bf38fbcea72f758d.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point $x_0$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of $x_0$. The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S$.\n- A point $x_0$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of $x_0$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S$.\nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set $M$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1930/Source/Images/22e313a10b473123c54290fd53624b0f.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1930/Source/Images/908766ecc45f4b98447e040aa693926d.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is *not* a boundary point since there exists any neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained *outside* the set $M$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1930/Source/Images/5e9a6b515bc2690bb38cae29d12982e7.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124117 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/id #uuid "1f21955b-70dc-5b61-8b19-42860de1be4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-11031" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $\\partial A$ are given by\n$A=\\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2: y > 4-2x\\big\\}, \\qquad \\partial A = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2: y = 4-2x\\big\\}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The point $P(1, 2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n2. The point $P(1, 2)$ belongs to $A$\n3. The point $Q(0,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0$. A *neighborhood* of a point $\\mathbf x_0$ is the set of points whose distance from $\\mathbf x_0$ is less than $\\delta$.\n$\\{\\mathbf x: \\mid \\mathbf x-\\mathbf x_0 \\mid < \\delta \\}$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ is the open disc $\\mid \\mathbf x - \\mathbf x_0 \\mid < \\delta$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1930/Source/Images/57e8a083558d146ab81cebb75f20ce42.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$. The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S$.\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S$.\nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S$.\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie above the line $y=4-2x$. A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1930/Source/Images/6d0b1d19e96a6c8d35556858a81a19ad.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,2)$ and $Q(0,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1930/Source/Images/0a5525784fe11c11eb5f968c8c095d5a.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(1, 2)$ satisfy the equation $y = 4-2x$. Hence, $P$ lies in the set $\\partial A$, the boundary of $A$.\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(1, 2)$ do not satisfy the inequality $y > 4-2x$. Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A$.\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124118 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :question/id #uuid "9b01032c-d4e6-5660-a3c9-477e67c011a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-11032" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $\\partial A$ are given by\n$A=\\bigg\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2: \\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{8} \\geq 1 \\bigg\\}, \\qquad \\partial A = \\bigg\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2: \\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{8} =1 \\bigg\\}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The point $Q\\left(-\\sqrt 2,2\\right)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n2. The point $P(1, 1)$ belongs to $A$\n3. The point $Q\\left(-\\sqrt 2,2\\right)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0$. A *neighborhood* of a point $\\mathbf x_0$ is the set of points whose distance from $\\mathbf x_0$ is less than $\\delta$.\n$\\{\\mathbf x: \\mid \\mathbf x-\\mathbf x_0 \\mid < \\delta \\}$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ is the open disc $\\mid \\mathbf x - \\mathbf x_0 \\mid < \\delta$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1930/Source/Images/1390e3f26b1a46ff0eeee85a7cce6edc.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$. The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S$.\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S$.\nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S$.\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or outside the ellipse\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{8} = 1\n$$\n\nA sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1930/Source/Images/c88b6acfea685d162a6a92b01da32622.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1, 1)$ and\n\n$$\nQ\\left(-\\sqrt 2,2\\right)\n$$\n\nand some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1930/Source/Images/80305176afa50ebc97ada5292dc94106.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $Q\\left(-\\sqrt 2,2\\right)$ satisfy the equation $\\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{8} = 1$. Hence, $P$ lies in the set $\\partial A$, the boundary of $A$.\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(1, 1)$ do not satisfy the inequality $\\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{8} \\geq 1$. Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A$.\n- Statement III is false. Every open neighborhood of $Q$ contains points that belong to $A$ and points that do not belong to $A$. Therefore, the point $Q$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ffe6a10-945f-5055-8704-77610ca7af6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121298} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b739bc-83a2-509c-8257-8d25508a0af5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121299} {:answer/id #uuid "e15e9680-a271-5911-bb6c-338b25f55e7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121300} {:answer/id #uuid "e243f6e6-5585-57ec-8a09-91149180f286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121301} {:answer/id #uuid "7dc271ad-d07a-5dd8-87ac-0db115d40bae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9b739bc-83a2-509c-8257-8d25508a0af5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121299} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c6d1461-4ff5-5329-ad4d-8ef22820d89e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121297}] :question/id #uuid "98524e7d-f97e-5125-b634-0488b239406e" :question/math-academy-id "q-156152" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1930/Source/Images/q-156152.png)\n\nConsider the set $[MATH: M = [- 2, 1)]$ and the points $A$, $B$, and $C$, shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n2. $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n3. $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124900 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8058fec-4c1b-5577-a9c5-30e05f3b47c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122423} {:answer/id #uuid "ad63ad5d-18ec-592c-867e-23df6091481d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122424} {:answer/id #uuid "280c1366-1754-55c1-86e0-33aecbe5ba68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122425} {:answer/id #uuid "3008cdb9-5f8b-5f1a-b6c6-b9d9bb8c310b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122426} {:answer/id #uuid "7d58f386-d12a-570a-a138-e2ed2c523734" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3008cdb9-5f8b-5f1a-b6c6-b9d9bb8c310b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122426} :answer-field/id #uuid "84d3f574-a9bc-5cec-9d17-e21f0b3b0bd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122422}] :question/id #uuid "560c5161-e608-551f-9c8f-70f39e328770" :question/math-academy-id "q-36432" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1930/Source/Images/q-36432.png)\n\nConsider the set $M$, depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n2. $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n3. $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$"}]}
